WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.740
Start a fault review by naming the loss precisely.

2
00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:07.400
One supply unit, one input feed, one shared distribution segment and one

3
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:10.060
control function can produce very different outcomes.

4
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.280
The redundancy label alone doesn't identify which equipment remains available.

5
00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.580
Draw the surviving path and apply its supported ratings.

6
00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:22.020
Then include thermal conditions and any operating restriction that follows the event.

7
00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:26.140
If the remaining capacity is below the requested workload, the design needs

8
00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:29.960
a defined response rather than an assumption that it will continue unchanged.

9
00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:32.340
Recovery is another operating case.

10
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:37.240
Restoring a source can introduce transitions, sharing changes or other conditions that

11
00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:38.460
the system must manage.

12
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:43.160
Define what evidence permits restoration and who authorizes it through the approved

13
00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:44.080
operating process.

14
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.760
Don't infer a live-service procedure from this conceptual review.

15
00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:51.480
The result should identify what the workload may do during the fault

16
00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.980
and after recovery, with a traceable technical basis for both.

